    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d12">

                <pb facs="0122" n="18" sameAs="frus:frus1933v02/pg18"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.4112a/52</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Great Britain (Bingham)</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 101</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1933-10-03">October 3,
                        1933</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department refers to its instruction
                    No. 450 of July 28, 1930, to despatch No. 1158 of August 25, 1930, and to
                    previous correspondence relating to the negotiation of a proposed treaty of
                    arbitration between the Government of the United States of America and the
                    Government of Great Britain, extending the policy of arbitration enunciated in
                    the convention signed at Washington, April 4, 1908,<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d12-f1"/> which expired by limitation on June 4, 1928.</p>
                <p>The Department desires the Embassy to ascertain informally whether the Foreign
                    Office has completed its preliminary consideration of the draft treaty and is
                    prepared to continue the negotiations.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
